<GuideArticle slug="where-to-stay-night-before-aruba-cruise"
  title="Where to stay the night before an Aruba cruise"
  metaTitle="Where to Stay the Night Before an Aruba Cruise (2027) — 5 Min from the Terminal"
  description="Flying in the day before your Aruba sailing? The case for sleeping in downtown Oranjestad, 400 m from the cruise terminal: distances, check-in times, luggage logistics and dinner on foot."
  kicker="Cruise · practical guide" faq={faq}>
  <p>Cruise lines tell you to arrive in Aruba the day before your sailing, and they're right: a delayed flight on embarkation day is the one mistake the ship won't wait for. The question is where to sleep that night — and the answer most people miss is that <strong>downtown Oranjestad puts you a 5-minute walk from the terminal</strong>, while the resort strips at Palm Beach and Eagle Beach put you a 15–25 minute taxi ride away on a morning when everyone else wants the same taxi.</p>
  <h2>The geography, in plain numbers</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The Oranjestad cruise terminal sits on the harbour at the north end of downtown.</li>
    <li>Casa Playa — the former Royal Plaza Mall, the pink landmark every ship photographs — is about <strong>400 metres away: a 5-minute walk</strong> on flat pavement, luggage wheels and all.</li>
    <li>Queen Beatrix International Airport (AUA) to downtown Oranjestad is about 5 km — a <strong>10-minute drive</strong>, roughly $30–45 by arranged transfer.</li>
    <li>Palm Beach hotels are 7–8 km from the terminal; with cruise-morning traffic, plan 20–30 minutes plus the taxi wait.</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>The night-before evening, done right</h2>
  <p>Staying downtown means the pre-cruise evening is an asset instead of a layover. Oranjestad's restaurant district starts at the door — dozens of places within a 10-minute walk, from waterfront fish to Aruban stoba. You'll watch your ship arrive in port from the street corner. In the morning: coffee on the balcony, an unhurried shower, and a five-minute walk to the gangway while the Palm Beach crowd is still in traffic.</p>
  <h2>An apartment beats a hotel room the night before 10 days at sea</h2>
  <p>A full apartment lets you repack properly across a real floor, run a load of laundry (there's a washer in the unit), and refrigerate nothing you'll regret. Early check-in and late checkout exist for exactly these mornings — $45 each when the calendar allows.</p>
  <h2>Coming home through Aruba</h2>
  <p>One more Aruba-specific advantage: Queen Beatrix has <strong>US preclearance</strong>. If you fly home from Aruba after your cruise, you clear US immigration and customs before boarding and land as a domestic passenger — no arrival queue after a long travel day.</p>
</GuideArticle>
